      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of the certificate.\n\nCertificate-level:\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01, revision 9\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 2563125\n- Page total: 13\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 22 September 2023\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1 (from \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\")\n\nIssuing authority:\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V.\n- Address lines: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n- Person title: Certification Board? Actually \"Certification Board\" appears after the date. The person responsible is M.Ph.D. Schmidt. Title might be \"Certification Board\" — ambiguous. I could include person_title: \"Certification Board\" or omit. Let me include it as it appears to be the signer's role context. Actually the structure is: \"Issuing Authority NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1 / 22 September 2023 / Certification Board / NMi Certin B.V. ...\". Hmm, \"Certification Board\" might be the title. I'll include person_title: \"Certification Board\".\n\nApplicants:\n- ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n- Note: table says \"Applicant and Manufacturer\" — so this is both applicant and manufacturer.\n\nManufacturers:\n- Same: ABB Limited.\n\nCertified type:\n- Category: Water meter (electromagnetic water meter) — category examples include \"Water meter\"? The schema examples: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\", ... So \"Water meter\".\n- Type designations: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*. With footnote: X being 1 for standard, 3 for advanced, 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant; Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\n\nThe tables 1, 2 give general characteristics for FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z (Table 1) and FEW45Y.Z (Table 2). Table 3 gives software ID for FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z. Table 4 is for FEW45Y.Z but content cut off (this fragment ends there).\n\nModel family: The cert covers variants. Models: AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z (different X = 1, 3, 5). Also FET4* variants. Since Table 1 vs Table 2 differ, this is a model family.\n\nFamily name: AquaMaster4.\n\nModels: \n- FEW41Y.Z (standard)\n- FEW43Y.Z (advanced)\n- FEW45Y.Z (premium, mobile comms)\n- FET4* (sensor only? Y being 1,2,8,9 integral/remote version optionally sensor only — FET = sensor only likely)\n\nGiven the tables distinguish FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z together (Table 1) and FEW45Y.Z (Table 2), I'll define models: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z, FET4XY.Z? Hmm. FET4* is \"sensor only\" variant perhaps. Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only). So FEW vs FET: FEW = flow transmitter versions? FET = ... Actually AquaMaster4 FEW is combined? Not sure. Let me just record type_designations as [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4XY.Z\"] or literally \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\".\n\nType designations: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4XY.Z\" — but the markdown says \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4^*\". I'll record type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4XY.Z\"] — hmm, the * applies to both? \"FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\" — FET4 followed by *. The footnote explains X, Y, Z placeholders. I'll write type_designations as [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4XY.Z\"]? To be safe, maybe just [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4XY.Z\"]. Hmm, FET4* with X, Y, Z also placeholders: FET4XY.Z presumably. I'll use \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\" — but the markdown says \"FET4*\". Let me keep as given: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\"]? Risky fabrication. The safest: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4XY.Z\"]. Hmm.\n\nActually the pattern: FEW4XY.Z where X ∈ {1,3,5}, Y ∈ {1,2,8,9}, Z ∈ {R,V,F}. FET4* — FET4 followed by more characters. Given footnote says X being..., Y being..., Z being..., it applies to the whole type family. I'll include type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4XY.Z\"] and put the explanation in description.\n\nModel family models: based on Table 1 & 2 headings:\n- \"AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z\" (X=1 standard)\n- \"AquaMaster4 FEW43Y.Z\" (X=3 advanced)\n- \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\" (X=5 premium)\n\nMaybe also FET4 variants, but tables don't mention them. I'll include the three FEW models. Actually maybe I should note FET4* as sensor-only. To avoid fabrication, models: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z.\n\nHmm, but the certificate covers FET4* too. The tables only cover FEW variants. Table 4 is cut off. I'll include models FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z with labels standard/advanced/premium respectively.\n\nCharacteristics:\n\nType level (common across all): Actually Table 1 and Table 2 differ in electromagnetic environment, power supply, checking facilities presence. Common ones:\n- measuring_principle: Electromagnetic\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" — string\n- environmental_class: O (installed outdoors)\n- mechanical_class: M1\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min: -25, max: 55} °C\n- water_temperature_class: T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C)\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_map: 1.6 MPa (16 bar) — value 1.6, unit MPa\n- orientation: All positions\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: U0 and D0, except DN250+: U3 and D0 — this could be config_level.\n- pressure_loss_class: varies by size (DN40/50, >DN50, DN65–200, DN250+) and bore type (reduced vs full bore) — this is config_level with axis \"bore type and size\".\n\nModel level (differing between Table 1 and Table 2):\n- electromagnetic_environment: E2 (Table 1: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z) vs E1/E2 for FEW45Y.Z depending on external antenna cable. For FEW45: \"E1 for transmitter with external antenna cable; E2 for transmitter without external antenna cable\" — that's config_level within model. I could make model_level entries: electromagnetic_environment: FEW41Y.Z: E2, FEW43Y.Z: E2, FEW45Y.Z: E1/E2 depending... complicated. \n\nLet me structure:\n\ncharacteristics:\n- type_level:\n  - measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\"}\n  - accuracy_class: {value: \"1 and 2\"} — hmm, accuracy class is also in recommendation. Keep as string \"1 and 2\".\n  - environmental_class: {value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"} — maybe value \"O\", with note. I'll use {value: \"O\", ...}? The value includes the parenthetical. Structured value rule: value can be string. I'll do {value: \"O\"} and maybe description... Simpler: {value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"}.\n  - electromagnetic_environment: E2 for Table 1; but Table 2 differs → model_level.\n  - mechanical_class: {value: \"M1\"}\n  - temperature_range_ambient: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"} — note \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\". Could add footnote? It's inline, keep as value string? I'll add the note maybe as part of value... No — numeric range. I'll keep {min:-25, max:55}, unit °C. Could add a footnote text? The parenthetical is a clarification; I could add footnote to footnotes[]? Footnotes are for markers. I'll leave it.\n  - water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"} plus range? Water temperature class T50 with range +0.1°C to +50°C. I'd record water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"} and maybe water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C. Both fine.\n  - maximum_admissible_pressure_map: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} — also 16 bar. Value: 1.6, unit MPa. Maybe add alternative? Keep 1.6 MPa.\n  - orientation: {value: \"All positions\"}\n  - reverse_flow: {value: \"intended to measure reverse flow\"} — string.\n- model_level:\n  - electromagnetic_environment: FEW41Y.Z: \"E2\", FEW43Y.Z: \"E2\", FEW45Y.Z: \"E1/E2 (depends on antenna cable)\". Hmm — better: for FEW45, it's config dependent. I'll record model_level electromagnetic_environment with values E2, E2, and for FEW45Y.Z value \"E1 or E2 (see config)\". Alternatively config_level: axis \"external antenna cable\", attribute electromagnetic_environment. Let me do:\n    - model_level: {attribute: \"electromagnetic_environment\", values: [{model: \"AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"}, {model: \"AquaMaster4 FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"}, {model: \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\", value: \"E1 with external antenna cable / E2 without\"}]}\n  - checking_facilities: Table 1 has it on page 3 (applies to Table 1 models): \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"; Table 2 also has Type P. So both — type_level: checking_facilities: {value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"}. Both have it, so type_level.\n  - power_supply differs: Table 1: AC mains 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz; DC powered 6–32 V; replaceable battery 2×3.6 V (range 2.9–3.6 V). Table 2: AC mains including 4×3.6V batteries; DC including 4×3.6V; battery 6×3.6V.\n  \n  Power supply structure per model:\n  - ac_mains_voltage: {min: 95, max: 240} V\n  - ac_frequency: {min:50, max:60} Hz\n  - dc_supply_voltage: {min: 6, max: 32} V\n  - battery_count: 2 (Table1) / 6 (Table2), battery voltage 3.6 V, operating range 2.9–3.6 V.\n  - batteries_included_with_ac/dc: 4×3.6V for FEW45.\n\n  I'll do model_level entries:\n  - replaceable_battery_count: FEW41Y.Z: 2, FEW43Y.Z: 2, FEW45Y.Z: 6 (unit: \"×3.6 V cells\"? unit null, value number)\n  - replaceable_battery_voltage: 3.6 V all models → could be type_level.\n  - replaceable_battery_operating_range: {min 2.9, max 3.6} V → type_level.\n  - ac_mains_voltage: 95–240 V all → type_level.\n  - power_supply_type: AC mains, DC renewable → type_level.\n  - Also for FEW45: \"including 4X 3.6 V replaceable batteries\" for AC and DC supplies — that's a config detail; maybe skip or include as string in model-level attribute \"batteries_included_with_mains_or_dc_supply\": 4.\n\nHmm, keep it reasonable. Let me do model_level:\n1. electromagnetic_environment\n2. replaceable_battery_count: 2, 2, 6\n3. batteries_included_with_external_power: for FEW45: 4; for others: not stated → could omit for others or null. Schema requires value objects. Maybe simpler to record as string for FEW45 only: value \"4 × 3.6 V replaceable batteries included\". Values array needs entries per model though; entries can be subset? In R137 example, all models listed. I'll include all with FEW41/43 as null? Rules say don't fabricate; null means missing — acceptable? Might be safer to omit this attribute entirely or only mention in config... I'll omit it from model_level and just note battery count. Actually I could include it with value null for the other models. Hmm, I'll include with null values — it's informative. Actually rules say \"Empty strings are not values — use null or omit.\" So null is allowed.\n\nConfig level:\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: axis \"pipe size (full bore)\" — U0/D0 for < DN250; U3/D0 for DN250+. \n  config_level entry: {attribute: \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", axis: \"full bore size\", values: [{condition: \"smaller than DN250\", value: \"U0 (upstream) / D0 (downstream)\"}, {condition: \"DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 (upstream) / D0 (downstream)\"}]}\n  The schema example: values: {condition, value}. Values should be structured {condition, value, unit_symbol}. Value here is string \"U0 and D0\" — string ok.\n\n- pressure_loss_class: axis bore type + size.\n  values:\n  - {condition: \"reduced bore, DN40–DN50\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}\n  - {condition: \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\"}\n  - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN40–DN50\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}\n  - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN65–DN200\", value: \"Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\"}\n  - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN250 and larger\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}\n  Should value be structured with unit? {condition, value} per rule 5: `{condition, value, unit_symbol}`. I'll give value objects: {condition: ..., value: {min: null, max: 0.40}, unit_symbol: \"bar\"} plus the class label... The pressure loss class Δp 40 corresponds to 0.40 bar. Maybe value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\" as string is cleaner, or structured: {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 0.4}, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"} with condition \"reduced bore, DN40–DN50 (pressure loss class Δp 40)\". I'll use structured numeric: value {min: null, max: 0.4}, unit \"bar\", condition \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\". That loses \"Δp 40\" class label; add to condition. Good.\n\n- electromagnetic_environment for FEW45: axis \"external antenna cable\": E1 with, E2 without. I could put this in config_level with model mentioned in condition. Or model_level with string. I'll do config_level: {attribute: \"electromagnetic_environment\", axis: \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z: external antenna cable\", values: [{condition: \"with external antenna cable\", value: \"E1\"}, {condition: \"without external antenna cable\", value: \"E2\"}]}.\n\nSoftware tables: matrix_tables? Table 3 is software identification — could be matrix_tables entry: name \"Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\", columns: software_part_code, software_version, crc_checksum, remarks. Rows: many. Let me extract:\n\nPart code 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality):\n- 03.00.03 / B26AFE6B / -\n- 03.00.04 / 6E98963A / -\n- 03.02.00 / E12E5C04 / -\n- 03.03.02 / D4D1E40B / \"HMI display bug fixes. Low flow output bug fix.\"\n- 03.03.03 / CEEF45F1 / \"Modbus communication bug fix.\"\n- 03.03.06 / DAC51328 / -\n- 03.04.00 / 277BA336 / -\n\nPart code 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater):\n- 02.00.02 / 84EBD1C8 / -\n\nPart code 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater):\n- 02.00.01 / C28EAA16 / -\n\nTable 4 is cut off (just header, no rows). Skip or include empty? The header says \"Table 4 Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant [1]\". There's a footnote marker [1] on the table title but no footnote definition visible in this fragment. Hmm — the [1] marker might refer to something. No footnote text present. I'll skip Table 4 (empty) but maybe note? Don't fabricate — skip.\n\nRecommendation: R 49-1 (2013). Canonical id \"R49\", edition 2013? The recommendation is R49-1 (2013). id: \"R49\" (canonical), edition: 2013, and part? The schema has id, edition, amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes. R 49-1 — part 1. I could set id \"R49\" and note part... maybe id \"R49-1\"? The instruction: canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For R49-1, canonical id could be \"R49\" with edition 2013. Hmm, the \"-1\" is the part. I'll use id \"R49\", edition 2013, and add \"part\"? Not in schema. I'll set id: \"R49\", edition: 2013. Accuracy classes: [\"1\", \"2\"]. Scheme: \"A\".\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-2254799-01, 2020-08-14, 235 pages, role: type_evaluation_report\n- NMi-2254799-03, 2020-09-30, 44 pages\n- NMi-2254799-04, 2020-12-24, 180 pages\n- NMi-2564429-01, 2021-08-02, 26 pages\n- NMi-2496280-01, 2021-08-26, 29 pages\n- NMi-2496280-02, 2021-11-23, 25 pages\n- NMi-2563125-01, 2023-09-22, 94 pages\n\nRole: \"the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". First one (matching project? Project number is 2563125; the report NMi-2563125-01 matches project). Which is the Type Evaluation Report? The certificate says \"represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report\". Not explicitly labeled per report. I'll mark all as \"test_report\" except... hmm. The original type evaluation likely NMi-2254799-01 (largest, 235 pages). But not explicitly stated. To be safe, use role \"test_report\" for all, or mark NMi-2254799-01 as type_evaluation_report? Rule 8: don't fabricate. The text: \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". I'll set all roles to \"test_report\". Hmm, but the certificate references \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\". Given ambiguity, all \"test_report\" is safer. Actually maybe I could label them \"evaluation_report\"? I'll go with \"test_report\" for all.\n\nDocumentation folder: no. T11851-7 — \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded in the Documentation folder no. T11851-7.\" That's a documentation_file in test_reports? test_reports role: documentation_file is an allowed role. Add {id: \"T11851-7\", role: \"documentation_file\"}. Date/pages not given — omit.\n\nRevision history: revision \"9\", date 22 September 2023. Changes not given. The number line: \"Number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 9\". Date issued 22 September 2023. revision_history: [{revision: \"9\", date: \"2023-09-22\"}]. Changes omitted (not present).\n\nCertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"2563125\"\n- page_total: 13\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2023-09-22\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n- revision: 9 → include in number? number field: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 9\"? I'll keep number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and revision in revision_history.\n\nFootnotes: The asterisk footnote about X/Y/Z: \"* With X being 1 for standard or 3 for advanced or 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant; Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\" This is a footnote with marker \"*\". Add to footnotes: {marker: \"*\", text: \"...\"}. And Table 4 marker \"[1]\" with no text — skip (don't fabricate).\n\nType designations & description:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\"]? Original: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\". I'll write [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4XY.Z\"]. Hmm — FET4* could mean FET4 followed by anything. To stay literal: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4XY.Z\". I think FET4XY.Z is a reasonable expansion given the X/Y/Z footnote applies. But risk. Alternatively: type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\"] and description mentions literal. I'll go with [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\"]. Hmm wait, the footnote \"* With X being...\" applies to both FEW4XY.Z (no star needed since placeholders explicit) and FET4*. So FET4* = FET4XY.Z most likely. I'll use \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\". Actually, safer to preserve exactly: \"FET4*\". The schema says all type/model identifiers mentioned. I'll include [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\"] — no wait. Let me just be literal-ish: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4XY.Z\"]. The star expansion: FET4 + X + Y + Z presumably = FET4XY.Z. Given Y \"optionally sensor only\" — FET might be the sensor-only. I'll go with \"FET4XY.Z\".\n\n- module_designation: Not applicable? Not stated. The instrument is a water meter; components: flow sensor and flow transmitter mentioned. Not explicitly a module designation. Omit or null. I'll omit.\n\n- description: \"Electromagnetic water meter\" — from identification. Include description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\".\n\nComponents: Table 1 mentions \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\" — implies composition of flow sensor + flow transmitter. Also \"transmitter with/without external antenna cable\". Could record components: flow sensor, flow transmitter. But type designations not given for these. The schema: include only if cert names sub-assemblies. It names \"flow sensor\" and \"flow transmitter\" generically. I think it's borderline; I'll include components with role \"flow sensor\" and \"flow transmitter\" with empty type_designations? Schema requires type_designations[]; could be empty arrays. Hmm, rule 8 says omit if not present. The roles are named. I'll include:\n- {role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: []}\n- {role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: []}\nMaybe helpful. But could be noise. I'll include them lightly — actually I'll skip to avoid clutter? The power supply table mentions \"flow transmitter\". I think including is fine and informative. I'll include with empty type_designations.\n\nModel family:\n- family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- models:\n  - {model_id: \"AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z\", label: \"standard (X=1)\", attributes: {}}\n  - {model_id: \"AquaMaster4 FEW43Y.Z\", label: \"advanced (X=3)\"}\n  - {model_id: \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\", label: \"premium with mobile Comms MC (X=5)\"}\n  - FET4*? Not in tables. I'll leave models to the three FEW ones since tables are per-model. But cert covers FET4 too... The characteristics tables only distinguish FEW41/43 vs FEW45. I'll include the three.\n\nModel level attributes:\n\n1. electromagnetic_environment:\n   - FEW41Y.Z: \"E2\"\n   - FEW43Y.Z: \"E2\"\n   - FEW45Y.Z: \"E1 (with external antenna cable) / E2 (without)\" — string.\n   Structured values: {value: \"E2\"} etc.\n\n2. replaceable_battery_count:\n   - FEW41Y.Z: 2\n   - FEW43Y.Z: 2\n   - FEW45Y.Z: 6\n   unit: \"cells\" — unit_symbol: null; value number. Add attribute replaceable_battery_voltage type-level 3.6 V.\n\n3. batteries_included_with_external_power_supply:\n   - FEW41Y.Z: null? Not mentioned → omit attribute for those models. If I include, values: [{model FEW41, value null}, ...]. I'll include with null for 41/43 and \"4 x 3.6 V\" for 45? That seems odd. Better: attribute \"batteries_included_with_mains_or_dc_supply\" with FEW45Y.Z: {value: 4} and others omitted. Schema values[] is a list; I can list only FEW45? The R137 example lists all models. I'll include all three with null for the first two. Acceptable.\n\nHmm, actually simpler: skip this detail? It's meaningful (FEW45 includes 4×3.6V batteries with AC or DC supply). I'll include.\n\n4. software table reference differs (table 3 vs table 4) — not a characteristic per se.\n\nType-level characteristics:\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\"}\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"1 and 2\"} — string. Or values [1,2]? Keep string.\n- environmental_class: {value: \"O\"} plus note \"installed outdoors\". I'll do {value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"}? Rules prefer structured but strings allowed. I'll use value \"O\" and add attribute environmental_class_note? Overkill. Use {value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"}.\n- mechanical_class: {value: \"M1\"}\n- temperature_range_ambient: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"} — from \"(+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\".\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} — attribute \"maximum_admissible_pressure_map\". Also equals 16 bar; skip second.\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"} → value \"All positions\".\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\"} → maybe boolean-ish: value \"intended to measure reverse flow\". Keep as string.\n- checking_facilities: {value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"}\n- replaceable_battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- replaceable_battery_operating_range: {value: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- ac_mains_supply_voltage: {value: {min: 95, max: 240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- ac_mains_supply_frequency: {value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n- dc_supply_voltage: {value: {min: 6, max: 32}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"AC mains, DC renewable energy, replaceable battery\"} — maybe skip; the individual entries cover it. I'll skip.\n\nConfig level:\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class (axis: pipe size):\n  - {condition: \"full bore size smaller than DN250\", value: \"U0 (upstream) and D0 (downstream)\"}\n  - {condition: \"full bore size DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 (upstream) and D0 (downstream)\"}\n- pressure_loss_class (axis: bore type and nominal size):\n  - {condition: \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: {max: 0.40}, unit bar, note Δp 40}. I'll include class label in condition: \"reduced bore, DN40–DN50 (class Δp 40)\". value {min: null, max: 0.4}, unit \"bar\".\n  - {condition: \"reduced bore, larger than DN50 (class Δp 63)\", value {max: 0.63}, bar}\n  - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN40–DN50 (class Δp 10)\", value {max: 0.10}, bar}\n  - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN65–DN200 (class Δp 16)\", value {max: 0.16}, bar}\n  - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN250 and larger (class Δp 10)\", value {max: 0.10}, bar}\n- electromagnetic_environment (axis: FEW45Y.Z external antenna cable):\n  - {condition: \"with external antenna cable\", value: \"E1\"}\n  - {condition: \"without external antenna cable\", value: \"E2\"}\n  But this is model-specific... config_level doesn't have model field. I'll fold model into axis: axis: \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z: external antenna cable presence\". OK.\n\nWait — should I duplicate electromagnetic_environment in model_level AND config_level? That's redundant. Options: model_level with string \"E1 or E2 (with/without external antenna cable)\" and skip config entry. Or config_level only with model in axis. I'll do model_level values: FEW41: E2, FEW43: E2, FEW45: null? Hmm.\n\nSimplest coherent: model_level entry:\n{attribute: \"electromagnetic_environment\", values: [\n  {model: \"AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"},\n  {model: \"AquaMaster4 FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"},\n  {model: \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\", value: \"E1 / E2 (with external antenna cable: E1; without: E2)\"}\n]}\nThat keeps it in one place. Good enough; skip config entry for EM environment.\n\nMatrix tables: Table 3 software identification:\n{\n  name: \"Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z and AquaMaster4 FEW43Y.Z\",\n  columns: [\"software_part_code\", \"software_part_description\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\", \"remarks\"],\n  rows: [\n    {software_part_code: \"3KXF208402U0113\", software_part_description: \"Full functionality\", software_version: \"03.00.03\", crc_checksum: \"B26AFE6B\", remarks: null},\n    ... etc\n  ]\n}\nRows repeat part code per row. Good.\n\nAlso Table 4 header exists but no rows — mention? Could add matrix_table with empty rows? No — omit; the fragment ends mid-table. Actually \"Table 4 Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant [1]\" then fragment ends. Content on page 4+ presumably continues in later parts. Since this is part 1 of 4, later fragments will have it. For THIS fragment, omit.\n\nFootnotes:\n- marker \"*\", text: \"With X being 1 for standard or 3 for advanced or 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant; Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"\n- marker \"[1]\" — no definition → skip? Rule 7 says extract footnote definitions. No definition here. Skip.\n\nIssuing authority person_title: The layout: \"Issuing Authority NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1 / 22 September 2023 / Certification Board / NMi Certin B.V. ...\" — \"Certification Board\" likely the body signing. I'll set person_title: \"Certification Board\". Reasonable.\n\nDate formats: use ISO \"2023-09-22\".\n\nTest report dates: 2020-08-14, 2020-09-30, 2020-12-24, 2021-08-02, 2021-08-26, 2021-11-23, 2023-09-22.\n\nAccuracy classes in recommendation: [\"1\", \"2\"].\n\nAlso certificate includes \"revision\": put in revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"9\", \"date\": \"2023-09-22\", \"changes\": null}]. changes omitted.\n\nNow components: flow sensor + flow transmitter. Also \"external antenna cable\" as accessory for FEW45 transmitter. I'll keep two components.\n\nLet me now also double check \"Software identification\" as characteristic: \"See details in table 3\" — skip.\n\nPressure loss class values: Δp 40 means 0.40 bar at... For water meters, Δp class = pressure loss at Q3 in bar×100? Whatever — record as given.\n\nMAP: 1.6 MPa (16 bar). value 1.6, unit MPa. I'll record just MPa.\n\nNow build JSON.\n\nAlso \"Power supply\" for FEW45 includes \"including 4X 3.6 V replaceable batteries\" — attribute batteries_supplied_with_external_power: model_level FEW45: 4. I'll add model_level entry \"replaceable_batteries_included_with_mains_or_dc_supply\" values: FEW41 null, FEW43 null, FEW45 {value: 4}. Hmm null values... The schema example R137 lists all. I'll include; it's honest.\n\nActually, wait: for FEW41/43 the text doesn't say batteries are included with AC/DC; only FEW45 says \"including 4X 3.6 V replaceable batteries\". For 41/43 battery is standalone option (2x 3.6V). So null for 41/43 is correct-ish (not stated). Fine.\n\nBattery voltage: 3.6 V each (both tables). type_level.\n\nLet me write the JSON now.\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"2563125\",\n    \"page_total\": 13,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2023-09-22\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Certification Board\",\n    \"phone\": \"+31 88 636 2332\",\n    \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n    \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"ABB Limited\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"ABB Limited\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4XY.Z\"],\n    \"module_designation\": null,\n    \"description\": \"An electromagnetic water meter\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"AquaMaster4\",\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z\", \"label\": \"standard (X=1)\"},\n      {\"model_i